You asked for proof of residence. It is on its way by post.

Three headers here are doing the work.

`To:` is the plus-address zaakpost+antwoord@valtimo.local. The receive-mail link on the
`await-reply` receive task filters on recipientContains = "zaakpost+antwoord@valtimo.local",
so this mail is offered to that task. The link on the message start event filters on
recipientContains = "zaakpost@valtimo.local", which this address does NOT contain - the
"+antwoord" sits in between - so the same mail does not also create a new case.

`References` names <fixture-01-plain-text@example.org>, the Message-ID of 01-plain-text.eml
and therefore the value of mailMessageId on the case that mail started. That is what decides
WHICH waiting case is resumed: the filter above only says the mail is a reply, the reference
says whose. Strip this header and the mail resumes nothing at all - by design, since the
alternative is delivering it to every case parked at `await-reply`.

That split matters because ReceiveMailProperties can only express positive, AND-ed
"contains" filters. There is no way to say "subject contains Aanvraag but not Re:", so
separating intake from replies has to be done on an address the two filters disagree about.

`X-Fixture-Envelope-To` is not a real mail header; ./dev.sh send reads it and uses it as the
SMTP envelope recipient. Without it the mail would be delivered to a separate
zaakpost+antwoord mailbox instead of landing in the INBOX the plugin polls, which is exactly
what an alias or catch-all does on a real server.
